Let \( n \) be the number of quizzes Maureen has taken so far, and let \( \mu \) be the current mean of her quiz scores. The total sum of her current scores is \( S = n \mu \).

First scenario: If Maureen scores 11 on the next quiz, her mean will increase by 1. This gives the equation:
\[
n \mu + 11 = (n + 1)(\mu + 1)
\]
Expanding and simplifying:
\[
n \mu + 11 = n \mu + \mu + n + 1 \implies 11 = \mu + n + 1 \implies \mu + n = 10
\]

Second scenario: If Maureen scores 11 on each of the next three quizzes, her mean will increase by 2. This gives the equation:
\[
n \mu + 33 = (n + 3)(\mu + 2)
\]
Expanding and simplifying:
\[
n \mu + 33 = n \mu + 2n + 3\mu + 6 \implies 33 = 2n + 3\mu + 6 \implies 2n + 3\mu = 27
\]

We now have the system of equations:
1. \( \mu + n = 10 \)
2. \( 2n + 3\mu = 27 \)

Express \( n \) in terms of \( \mu \) from the first equation:
\[
n = 10 - \mu
\]

Substitute into the second equation:
\[
2(10 - \mu) + 3\mu = 27 \implies 20 - 2\mu + 3\mu = 27 \implies 20 + \mu = 27 \implies \mu = 7
\]

Thus, the mean of her quiz scores currently is \boxed{7}.
