Alicia and Beth are biking towards each other from cities A and B, which are 45 miles apart. Alicia bikes at 18 mph, and Beth bikes at 12 mph. To find the distance from City A where they will meet, we first calculate their combined speed:

\[
\text{Combined speed} = 18 \, \text{mph} + 12 \, \text{mph} = 30 \, \text{mph}
\]

Next, we determine the time it takes for them to meet:

\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Combined speed}} = \frac{45 \, \text{miles}}{30 \, \text{mph}} = 1.5 \, \text{hours}
\]

In this time, Alicia will have traveled:

\[
\text{Distance}_\text{Alicia} = 18 \, \text{mph} \times 1.5 \, \text{hours} = 27 \, \text{miles}
\]

Thus, they will meet \boxed{27} miles from City A.
