Mrs. Jones has four identical glasses and wants to pour orange juice into each of them equally. She filled the first three glasses completely (1 unit each) and the fourth glass to one-third (1/3 unit). 

First, we calculate the total amount of juice:
\[ 1 + 1 + 1 + \frac{1}{3} = 3 + \frac{1}{3} = \frac{10}{3} \text{ units} \]

She wants to distribute this equally among the four glasses. Each glass should have:
\[ \frac{10}{3} \div 4 = \frac{10}{12} = \frac{5}{6} \text{ units} \]

Each of the first three glasses needs to pour out:
\[ 1 - \frac{5}{6} = \frac{1}{6} \text{ units} \]

The fourth glass needs to receive:
\[ \frac{5}{6} - \frac{1}{3} = \frac{5}{6} - \frac{2}{6} = \frac{3}{6} = \frac{1}{2} \text{ units} \]

Since each of the first three glasses pours out \(\frac{1}{6}\) unit, the total poured into the fourth glass is:
\[ 3 \times \frac{1}{6} = \frac{3}{6} = \frac{1}{2} \text{ units} \]

Thus, the fraction of a glass that must be poured from each of the first three glasses into the fourth glass is \(\frac{1}{6}\). Therefore, \(m = 1\) and \(n = 6\), and \(m + n = 1 + 6 = 7\).

\[
\boxed{7}
\]
