To determine Charlie's unicycle speed in inches per second, we start by calculating the circumference of the wheel. The radius of the wheel is 9 inches, so the circumference is:

\[
2 \pi \times 9 = 18\pi \text{ inches}
\]

Next, we know the wheel makes 2 revolutions every 3 seconds. Therefore, the number of revolutions per second is:

\[
\frac{2 \text{ revolutions}}{3 \text{ seconds}} = \frac{2}{3} \text{ revolutions per second}
\]

Each revolution covers a distance of \(18\pi\) inches. Thus, the speed in inches per second is:

\[
\left(\frac{2}{3} \text{ revolutions per second}\right) \times (18\pi \text{ inches per revolution}) = \frac{2 \times 18\pi}{3} = 12\pi \text{ inches per second}
\]

Therefore, Charlie's unicycle speed is \(\boxed{12\pi}\) inches per second.
