To find the value of \( a \) that makes the function \( f(n) \) continuous, we need to ensure that the left-hand limit and the right-hand limit at \( n = a \) are equal to each other and to \( f(a) \).

The function is defined as:
\[
f(n) =
\begin{cases}
4n + 3 & \text{if } n < a, \\
7n - 12 & \text{if } n \geq a.
\end{cases}
\]

For continuity at \( n = a \), we need:
\[
\lim_{n \to a^-} f(n) = \lim_{n \to a^+} f(n) = f(a)
\]

First, compute the left-hand limit as \( n \) approaches \( a \):
\[
\lim_{n \to a^-} f(n) = 4a + 3
\]

Next, compute the right-hand limit as \( n \) approaches \( a \):
\[
\lim_{n \to a^+} f(n) = 7a - 12
\]

Set these two limits equal to each other:
\[
4a + 3 = 7a - 12
\]

Solving for \( a \):
\[
4a + 3 = 7a - 12 \\
3 = 3a - 12 \\
15 = 3a \\
a = 5
\]

Thus, the value of \( a \) that makes the function continuous is \(\boxed{5}\).
