Karla drove her vehicle 12,000 miles last year. To determine how many gallons of gasoline she would have saved by driving a hybrid car averaging 48 miles per gallon instead of an SUV averaging 15 miles per gallon, we need to calculate the gasoline usage for both cars and find the difference.

First, we calculate the gasoline used for the SUV:
\[
\text{Gallons used for SUV} = \frac{12,000 \text{ miles}}{15 \text{ mpg}} = 800 \text{ gallons}
\]

Next, we calculate the gasoline used for the hybrid:
\[
\text{Gallons used for hybrid} = \frac{12,000 \text{ miles}}{48 \text{ mpg}} = 250 \text{ gallons}
\]

The gasoline saved is the difference between the two amounts:
\[
\text{Gasoline saved} = 800 \text{ gallons} - 250 \text{ gallons} = 550 \text{ gallons}
\]

Thus, the gasoline saved would be \boxed{550} gallons.
