To determine the primes \( p \) for which the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) has no solutions, we need to check if 8 has a multiplicative inverse modulo \( p \). An integer \( a \) has a multiplicative inverse modulo \( p \) if and only if \( a \) and \( p \) are coprime. Since \( p \) is a prime, the only way 8 and \( p \) are not coprime is if \( p \) divides 8. The only prime that divides 8 is 2.

For \( p = 2 \), the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{2} \) simplifies to \( 0 \equiv 1 \pmod{2} \), which is not true. Therefore, there are no solutions for \( p = 2 \).

For any other prime \( p \) (which are all odd primes), 8 and \( p \) are coprime, meaning 8 has an inverse modulo \( p \). Thus, the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) will always have a solution for any odd prime \( p \).

Therefore, the only prime for which the congruence has no solution is \( p = 2 \). The sum of all such primes is 2.

